<div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">Brentwood UFSD Leadership Directory</h1>
    <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in Brentwood UFSD, Brentwood, Suffolk County.</p>

    <div class="contact-grid">

        <div class="contact-box">
            <h3>Special Education Office</h3>
            <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 District Office:</strong><br>Brentwood UFSD</div>
            <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:631-434-2401">631-434-2401</a></div>
            <div class="contact-item"><strong>📍 Mailing Address:</strong><br>52 Third Avenue, Brentwood, NY 11717</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
        <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYSED Special Education QA (SEQA).
    </div>


    <section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
        </h2>

        <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

            <div class="protocol-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>.
                </p>
                <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary.</li>
                    <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em></li>
                    <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates and names.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="protocol-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder:
                </p>
                <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call.</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal).</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent.</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="important-info" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Important Information for Brentwood UFSD Special Education Parents
        </h2>

        <div class="info-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(400px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

            <div>
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Understanding the CSE Process</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    The Committee on Special Education (CSE) is responsible for determining eligibility for special education services and developing Individualized Education Programs (IEPs) for students with disabilities. Familiarize yourself with the process.  Referrals can come from various sources, including parents, teachers, or doctors (8 NYCRR §200.4(a)). The CSE process involves referral, evaluation, IEP development, and annual review (8 NYCRR §200.4).
                </p>
                <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Referral:</strong> A written referral starts the process. Document everything!</li>
                    <li><strong>Evaluation:</strong> You have the right to an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense if you disagree with the district's evaluation (34 CFR §300.502). Requesting an IEE can be a powerful advocacy tool.</li>
                    <li><strong>IEP Development:</strong> The IEP must include measurable annual goals (8 NYCRR §200.4(d)(2)). Make sure the goals are specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound (SMART).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div>
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Parent Rights Under IDEA</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    The Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) provides significant rights to parents of children with disabilities. Key rights include the right to participate in all CSE meetings, the right to examine all records related to your child's education, and the right to due process if you disagree with the district's decisions (34 CFR §300.500 et seq.).
                </p>
                <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Prior Written Notice (PWN):</strong> The district *must* provide you with PWN whenever they propose or refuse to change your child's educational placement or services (34 CFR §300.503). Analyze PWNs carefully.</li>
                    <li><strong>Stay Put:</strong> During any dispute, your child has the right to "stay put" in their current educational placement until the issue is resolved (34 CFR §300.518).  Understand how to invoke "Stay Put."</li>
                    <li><strong>Due Process:</strong> If you disagree with the CSE's decisions, you have the right to file a due process complaint (34 CFR §300.507). Seek legal counsel before filing due process.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div>
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Common Pitfalls in Brentwood UFSD (and How to Avoid Them)</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    Navigating the special education system in any district can be challenging. Here are some common pitfalls and strategies to overcome them:
                </p>
                <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Inadequate Evaluations:</strong> Ensure evaluations are comprehensive and address all areas of concern. Request specific tests if needed.  If the district refuses, consider an IEE.</li>
                    <li><strong>Generic IEP Goals:</strong> Push for specific, measurable goals that are tailored to your child's individual needs. Vague goals are difficult to track and don't lead to meaningful progress.</li>
                    <li><strong>Placement Issues:</strong> Ensure the IEP placement aligns with your child's needs. Don't be afraid to advocate for a more appropriate setting, even if it's not the district's preferred option. Consider private placements if the public school cannot meet your child's needs.</li>
                    <li><strong>Lack of Communication:</strong> Maintain open and consistent communication with the CSE. Document all interactions and follow up in writing.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div>
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Advocacy Tips for Parents</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    Effective advocacy is crucial for ensuring your child receives the appropriate special education services.
                </p>
                <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Know Your Rights:</strong> Familiarize yourself with IDEA, New York State Part 200 Regulations, and district policies.</li>
                    <li><strong>Document Everything:</strong> Keep a detailed record of all communications, evaluations, and IEPs.</li>
                    <li><strong>Be Prepared for Meetings:</strong> Prepare an agenda, bring relevant documents, and take notes.</li>
                    <li><strong>Don't Be Afraid to Ask Questions:</strong> Clarify anything you don't understand.</li>
                    <li><strong>Bring an Advocate:</strong> Consider bringing a special education advocate or attorney to CSE meetings. Having a knowledgeable advocate can level the playing field.</li>
                    <li><strong>Build Relationships:</strong> While advocating fiercely, try to build a collaborative relationship with the school team.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="faq-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 20px;">

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #444; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is the difference between a 504 plan and an IEP?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">
                    A 504 plan provides accommodations for students with disabilities who do not require specialized instruction. An IEP, on the other hand, is for students who require specialized instruction and related services due to a disability.  IEPs are governed by IDEA, while 504 plans are governed by Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act. A student with a 504 plan may have ADHD, while a student with an IEP may have autism.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #444; margin-bottom: 5px;">How do I request an IEE?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">
                    If you disagree with the district's evaluation, you have the right to request an IEE at public expense.  Put your request in writing, stating the reasons for your disagreement with the district's evaluation. The district must either grant your request or initiate a due process hearing to demonstrate that their evaluation was appropriate (34 CFR §300.502).
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #444; margin-bottom: 5px;">What if my child's IEP is not being implemented?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">
                    If you believe your child's IEP is not being implemented, document the instances of non-compliance and communicate your concerns to the CSE. If the issues persist, you can file a state complaint with NYSED or request mediation or due process. The school is LEGALLY obligated to implement the IEP as written.
                </p>
            </div>

             <div class="faq-item">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #444; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is "Stay Put" and how do I invoke it?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">
                   "Stay Put" is your child's right to remain in their current educational placement while you and the school district resolve a dispute regarding their IEP. To invoke "Stay Put," you must formally disagree with the proposed change and usually file for mediation or due process. The "Stay Put" provision ensures that your child's education is not disrupted during the dispute resolution process (34 CFR §300.518). Ensure that you clearly state in writing that you are invoking "Stay Put."
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>

</div>
